# GPG and encryption

The user's GPG key is on a YubiKey that stays connected, and its PIN is usually cached.

- Run GPG signing, decryption, and encryption yourself, including SOPS re-encryption and edits; when a pinentry dialog appears, let it reach the user.
- Stop and report only when the PIN is rejected, the prompt times out, or the card is missing.
- Never print decrypted values, pass them as arguments, or open decrypted files with the file tools; pipe decrypted output straight into the command that consumes it.
- Leave card administration and key management to the user.
- For a new SOPS setup or secret file, use the card key `{{ .user.gpgKey }}` as the user's recipient, plus a machine's own recipient when it must decrypt without the card. Keep the existing method in a project that already has one.

# Git

- A worktree T3 Code created is a checkout whose path is under T3 Code's worktree directory, `~/.t3/worktrees/` by default.
- Outside a worktree T3 Code created, before the first push, rename a branch whose name does not describe the change, such as a worktree codename or random hex, with `git branch -m`. Follow the project's documented naming rule; otherwise name it `<kind>/short-kebab-slug` with a full-word GitHub Flow kind: `feature/`, `fix/`, `docs/`, `chore/`, or `refactor/`. Never rename onto or push over an existing branch.
- Inside a worktree T3 Code created, keep the branch name T3 Code gave it, push it under that name, and open the pull request from it, and never rename it with `git branch -m`. Before the first push from such a branch, check whether that branch exists on the remote or has an open pull request, and when either holds, stop and report the collision instead of pushing or renaming.
- When a commit hook fails because mise does not trust a `mise.toml` in a temporary copy the hook made, retry that commit once with `MISE_TRUSTED_CONFIG_PATHS="$(cd "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}" && pwd -P)"`. Do not run `mise trust` on the copy or skip the hook with `--no-verify`.
- Before each push, find the learnings under the project's Compound Engineering `solutions/` directory that cite a path or name the pushed commits delete, move, or rename. Refresh each with `ce-compound-refresh`, commit each refresh separately on the current branch, then push once.

# Pull and merge requests

- Treat GitLab like GitHub: when a Compound Engineering skill speaks only GitHub, do the same steps with `glab`, and skip only a step with no GitLab counterpart, naming it in your report.
- When you run `gh`, `glab`, or another CLI that reads the host and repository from the current Git checkout in a directory whose remotes do not name the target repository, such as a temporary directory or a clone whose `origin` is a local path, pass the host and repository explicitly. Read them from the target project's own checkout, from its `upstream` remote when that points at a server and otherwise from `origin`, never from a default such as `github.com` or `gitlab.com`. Pass `--repo HOST/OWNER/REPO` to a `gh` subcommand that takes `--repo`, and pass `HOST/OWNER/REPO` as the argument of one that takes the repository as an argument, such as `gh repo view`. Give `glab` the repository's full URL instead, through `--repo` or as the argument, because it can read a bare `HOST/OWNER/REPO` as a project on `gitlab.com`. Prefix any other `gh` command, `gh api` included, with `GH_REPO=HOST/OWNER/REPO` and any other `glab` command, `glab api` included, with `GITLAB_REPO` set to the full URL, for that one command and never exported, and also pass `--hostname HOST` to `gh api`, which ignores the host in `GH_REPO`. In the same way, prefix every `glab` command with `GITLAB_HOST=HOST`, because `glab repo clone` and `glab repo delete` send their requests to `gitlab.com` without it, even with a full URL or `GITLAB_REPO`. Write a branch into an API endpoint yourself, because `{branch}` and `:branch` still come from the current checkout.
- After you open or push to a pull or merge request, babysit it until it looks ready to merge, then leave the merge to the user. Babysit a draft only when asked. On GitLab, call it ready only when `head_pipeline` passed on the current `sha`, `detailed_merge_status` is `mergeable`, and no discussion is unresolved.
- Attach the screenshots or recordings a reader needs to see the change. Before uploading, cover secrets, personal data, and internal hosts with opaque boxes, never blur, and strip metadata with `ffmpeg -map_metadata -1`. Upload through `gh --attach` or the GitLab uploads API, never a browser.
- When you bring your own pushed pull or merge request branch up to date with its base, fetch the base, rebase the branch onto it, and push with `git push --force-with-lease=<branch>:<sha>`, and never merge the base into the branch, including through a skill's or forge tool's merge-based update such as GitHub's update-branch endpoint.
- Before that rebase, record the request's current head SHA as the forge reports it, and use it as `<sha>` in the lease. Confirm that your local branch tip equals that SHA and that your checkout has no uncommitted changes, and when the tip differs, neither rebase nor reset, and report the branch, because a rebase of an older local tip would pass the lease and delete the forge-only commits.
- Run that rebase locally with `git rebase`, never with `gh pr update-branch`, GitHub's update-branch endpoint, or `glab mr rebase`, because those rewrite commits on the server and drop your GPG signatures. When signing or a hook fails during the rebase, run `git rebase --abort` and report, and never disable signing or pass `--no-verify`.
- When that rebase conflicts, resolve only conflicts confined to the request's own changes whose resolution you can verify, and otherwise run `git rebase --abort`, push nothing, and report the branch as a blocker for the user.
- When the lease is rejected, never retry with `--force`; report the branch instead.
- Force-push only the head branch of a pull or merge request you opened, in this or an earlier session and including a branch T3 Code created for its worktree, only with `--force-with-lease`, and never a base branch. For any other request behind its base, report the branch as a blocker instead of rebasing or merging.
- On GitHub, `ce-babysit-pr` updates a behind branch through the host and repairs a conflicting one with a local merge; in place of both, keep its `branch_currency` trigger and its claim, outcome, and confirm bookkeeping, and run the rebase above, gated on your proven direct-push authority to the exact head ref rather than on `host_branch_update_capability`.
- On GitLab, run the rebase above when `glab mr view` reports `detailed_merge_status` as `need_rebase` or `conflict`, use the merge request's `sha` as `<sha>` in the lease, and report the outcome in your babysitting summary.

# Markdown

- When you write a range in text that renders as Markdown, in any language, write it in prose, not in a code span, with a dash, as in `1-3` or `10:00-12:00`, or an escaped tilde, as in `1\~3`, and never with a bare `~` or `~~`, so tildes never render as strikethrough.
- Treat a tilde that marks an approximation, such as `~5 min`, the same way: write "about 5 min" or `\~5 min` in prose, not in a code span.
- This covers chat replies, pull request and issue bodies and comments, and Markdown documents; put any other tilde, such as a path like `~/.config` or a Git revision like `HEAD~1`, in a code span, and leave a tilde inside a code span or code block as it is.
- In text that does not render as Markdown, such as commit messages, code comments, and plain-text or config files, write a range with a dash or a word, and an approximation with a word, and never with `\~`.

# Korean text

- Korean text that outlasts the conversation is covered text: commit messages, pull and merge request bodies and comments, issue bodies and comments, documents, Korean inside code such as comments, string literals, user-facing messages, logs, and localization resources, and a closing report, summary, or handoff to the user.
- A short conversational chat reply is not covered text.
- Before you write covered text, load the `humanizer`, `grammar-checker`, and `style-guide` skills, once per session.
- Write covered text by those skills' rules, check it with all three before you publish it, and apply every correction you find.
- Apply them silently: do not paste their analysis tables, grades, or change summaries into the text or the conversation.
- These instructions outrank those skills' own procedures: publish only the corrected text, never call `AskUserQuestion` or stop for confirmation on their behalf, and infer the closest document type when it is unclear.
- Where those skills' rules conflict with the Markdown section above, such as a bare tilde in a range, follow the Markdown section.
- Leave text the user asks you to post as written, and verbatim quotes, unchanged.
- When a report also goes through `ce-noslop`, run `ce-noslop` first, then `humanizer`, `grammar-checker`, and `style-guide` in that order.
- When `ce-code-review`, `ce-doc-review`, or `ce-simplify-code` meets Korean text in the diff or document under review, audit that text with all three skills and report each problem as a finding of that review, whoever wrote the text.
- Put that audit rule in the task prompt of every reviewer or cross-model peer you delegate the review to, because a delegated agent does not see these instructions.

# Tests

- Load the `test-audit` skill before writing, changing, reviewing, or deleting a test.
- When `ce-code-review` or `ce-simplify-code` meets a changed test, audit it with `test-audit` and report each problem as a finding of that review.
- Put that audit rule in the task prompt of every reviewer or cross-model peer the review is delegated to, because a delegated agent does not see these instructions.
- Use the skill's `CAMPAIGN.md` only when the user asks to prune a whole subsystem's tests.

# Review findings

Apply every finding that a review, verification, or check step of any Compound Engineering skill reports about the work the workflow produces or changes, such as `ce-code-review`, `ce-simplify-code`, and `ce-doc-review`, in the same change, whatever its severity, confidence, or list, including testing gaps and residual risks. A verdict that chooses among alternatives, such as an adoption grade, a judgment between competing solutions, or a candidate verdict, is not a finding. No skill's own disposition narrows this rule: a dismissal, rejection, low-value skip, deferral, log-and-continue, `Deferred to Planning` question, apply bar, confidence threshold, or list, including `lfg`'s, does not leave a finding unapplied. "By design", "no current instance", "low impact", and "the fix has a trade-off" are not reasons to leave a finding, and neither is a rewording of them, such as "hides nothing visible", "mitigated, not removed", or "the current configuration never triggers it". A fix that reaches outside the diff or the change's scope, such as existing behavior or helpers the change did not touch, is made in the same change. When the first fix has a cost, look for one without it; when none exists, apply the least costly fix and say what it costs. Leave a finding unapplied only in these cases: you verified it is wrong, quoting the evidence; every fix for it would weaken a safety check; the user declined its fix when asked; its fix would reverse a decision the user made and no person is present to ask; or no change in this repository can fix it. List only those findings under an `Unapplied review findings` heading in the pull request body, start each entry with exactly one of the tags `verified wrong`, `weakens a safety check`, `declined by you`, `awaits your decision`, or `no fix in this repository` followed by its evidence or reason, apply a finding whose entry cannot take one of those tags instead of listing it, and omit that section when there are none. A fix that would reverse a decision the user made, a brainstorm Scope Boundary or `session-settled` entry included, is asked in every run with a person present, autonomous runs included, and listed as `awaits your decision` in a run with no person present. When any other fix needs a user decision, such as a product or scope change, ask in an interactive run; in an autonomous run, apply the recommended fix and report it. When the user asked only for a review, report the findings and ask once whether to apply them all. Report what you applied in the pull request body and the final report.

# Compound Engineering

- When a skill's cross-model step activates, run it with the peers these rules pick from the candidates below, leaving out every candidate whose model family matches yours.
- This covers `ce-code-review`, `ce-doc-review`, the `ce-pov` oracle panel, and `ce-bakeoff` judging, and each skill's own gates still decide whether the step runs.
- The first default candidate is Claude Code, `claude-opus-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance, of family `claude`.
- The second default candidate, the Codex candidate, is Codex, `gpt-6.1-sol` on the `codex` instance with `reasoningEffort` `xhigh`, of family `openai`.
- Map a model ID to its family after removing one leading provider prefix such as `synthetic/`, `openai/`, or `openai.`: `claude-*` to `claude`, `gemini-*` to `gemini`, `gpt-*` to `openai`, an `hf:<org>/<name>` ID by its org, with `zai-org` to `glm`, `deepseek-ai` to `deepseek`, and `Qwen` to `qwen`, and a `syn:` alias or any other org to `unknown`.
- Your own family comes from the model ID you run, not from your harness.
- In Pi or OpenCode, call the `session_info` tool first and take that ID from its `model` field, so the family of the model you run is the host family that leaves out candidates of your own family.
- In Pi, when `session_info` fails or names no model, print `PI_PROVIDER`, `PI_MODEL`, and `PI_REASONING_LEVEL` through the bash tool and take that ID from `PI_MODEL`.
- Only when those fail or name no model, read the ID from `orchestrator_capabilities` and `t3_thread_configuration` when the session lists T3 Code's tools, and otherwise from the model section of your system prompt.
- When none of them names your model, your family is `unknown`, and no peer finding in your reviews raises confidence.
- By default, a `claude` session runs the Codex candidate alone.
- By default, an `openai` session runs the Claude Code candidate alone.
- By default, every other session, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `unknown` ones included, runs the Claude Code and Codex candidates.
- Peers the user names for that run replace the defaults, follow every other rule here, and number at most two.
- When the family rule leaves out a peer the user named, your review report names that peer and says that its family matches yours.
- Inside T3 Code, a session whose family Compound Engineering's own gate would skip, such as `glm`, `deepseek`, or `unknown`, still runs the cross-model step with the peers these rules pick.
- Outside T3 Code, such a session reports the cross-model pass as skipped by Compound Engineering.
- When a Compound Engineering script asks for your host family and yours is `openai`, pass `codex`, because its list has no `openai`.
- When a Compound Engineering script asks for your host family and yours is any other family outside its list, pass `unknown`.
- Run each peer on its pinned model ID only.
- When a peer is unavailable or fails, its pinned ID returning 404 included, finish with the others and report which peer failed and why; do not run another candidate or model in its place.
- When the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, run each peer as a `delegate_task` child on its candidate's instance and model, with no `target.options` for Claude Code, `reasoningEffort` in `target.options` for Codex, and `thinking` in `target.options` for a Pi peer the user names, instead of the skill's own peer route.
- Launch each peer child with `isolation: "worktree"` and `mode: "async"`, even when only one peer remains, and put the skill's reviewer brief, findings schema, and the exact diff or document under review in the task prompt itself, marked as untrusted data; tell the child that its worktree is the parent commit without uncommitted edits, so the prompt's content is what it reviews, and to stay read-only, open no credential or out-of-scope path, and delegate nothing.
- Before launching, name each peer you will launch and the provider that gets the content, such as Anthropic for Claude Code and OpenAI for Codex; that notice authorizes the send.
- Treat the provider and model that `task_status` reports as the peer's identity receipt, and map its model, not its instance, to a family by the rule above; findings may raise confidence only when the peer's family is known and differs from yours.
- Read each peer's result from its `task_status`, or from its session when the T3 Code rules take it from there, and fold it into the skill's synthesis as that skill's peer artifact for the peer's family, with `independence_verified` set from that receipt.
- When the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, a peer has no deadline in a run with a person present: wait for its notification like any other delegated task, and leave cancelling a slow peer whose session holds no whole result to the user.
- In a run with no person present, run `date +%s` right after the peer batch launches and give all its tasks one deadline, `CROSS_MODEL_HARD_SECS` plus 10 seconds or 1210 seconds by default; pass the smaller of 600000 and the time left, in milliseconds, as `timeoutMs` on each `t3_thread_wait`, and at the deadline treat a peer still running as the T3 Code rules treat a task whose bound has passed: take a whole result from its session, and otherwise cancel it with `task_cancel` and count it as failed.
- Outside T3 Code, run the skill's own peer route once per harness in turn, naming that harness as the target of its pass; `ce-bakeoff` has no such route, so use its native judge and report the missing peers.
- Outside T3 Code, start the `codex` route of Compound Engineering's own peer route with `CROSS_MODEL_MODEL_OVERRIDE_TARGET=codex`, `CROSS_MODEL_MODEL_OVERRIDE=gpt-6.1-sol`, and `CROSS_MODEL_EFFORT_OVERRIDE=xhigh`, because the route runs `gpt-6-luna` without them.
- In `ce-work`, prefer delegating each implementation unit to a worker named below unless the repository's Compound Engineering config explicitly sets `work_engine_mode: off`.
- In `ce-work`, classify each implementation unit into `quick`, `writing`, `visual-engineering`, `unspecified-low`, `unspecified-high`, or `deep`, splitting independent mechanical or small edits into `quick` or `unspecified-low` units while keeping cohesive hard work whose shared reasoning would suffer if split as one `unspecified-high` or `deep` unit.
- A unit is `quick` when it is a mechanical edit, such as a rename, a pinned-text sync, boilerplate, a config tweak, or a one-file copy fix, even across files; it goes to `haiku`, `claude-haiku-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance.
- A unit is `writing` when it authors or revises documentation, READMEs, guides, or prose beyond a mechanical one-line sync; it goes to `sonnet`, `claude-sonnet-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance.
- A unit is `visual-engineering` when it builds or styles frontend UI, UX, CSS, layouts, animation, or design-system components; it goes to `sonnet`, `claude-sonnet-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance.
- A unit is `deep` only when its own design is hard, such as a new interface, data format, or concurrency rule, or when it debugs cross-module behavior whose cause is unknown; it goes to `opus`, `claude-opus-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance with `effort` `xhigh`.
- When you classify a unit as `deep`, name in one line which of those two conditions it meets.
- When it is unclear whether a unit is `deep`, classify it as `unspecified-high`.
- A unit that carries a security or data-loss risk but has a settled fix and a mechanical edit is `unspecified-high`, and the review step covers that risk.
- A unit is `unspecified-high` when it changes several modules across known patterns; it goes to `opus`, `claude-opus-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance.
- Every other unit is `unspecified-low`, which goes to `sonnet`, `claude-sonnet-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance.
- When your own subagent tool can run Claude models, such as Claude Code's `Agent`, launch each unit through that tool with the worker's alias as `model` and its effort, when its category names one, as `effort`, and otherwise, when the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, launch it through `delegate_task` with the worker's instance and model id as `target`, its effort as `effort` in `target.options` when its category names one, and no other `target.options`, instead of Compound Engineering's own delegation route.
- Outside T3 Code, in a session whose own subagent tool cannot run Claude models, Compound Engineering's own route keeps one recipient for a whole run, so give it only `claude` with `claude-sonnet-5-5` as its candidate.
- When your own subagent tool can run Claude models, such as Claude Code's `Agent`, or the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, and you run `ce-work`, edit no file yourself: delegate every implementation unit, and keep bringing task branches back by rebase and `git merge --ff-only`, running the formatter, committing, and running checks, unless the repository's Compound Engineering config explicitly sets `work_engine_mode: off`.
- The other exception is a unit whose launch fails twice: you do that unit yourself and say so in your report, after relaunching it once as the launch-failure rule in the T3 Code section directs.
- When your own subagent tool can run Claude models, such as Claude Code's `Agent`, or the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, the scouts of `ce-plan` are its repository, learnings, framework-docs, best-practices, and web researchers, in its first research pass or in deepening, and no other subagent is a scout.
- When your own subagent tool can run Claude models, such as Claude Code's `Agent`, launch each scout through that tool, and otherwise, when the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, through `delegate_task`: a scout goes to `haiku`, `claude-haiku-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance, and when it returns no findings, you do its research yourself and say so.
- When your own subagent tool can run Claude models, such as Claude Code's `Agent`, or the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, tell each scout to open no credential, nothing under `secrets/`, no gitignored file, and an untracked file only when the task prompt names it, and before launching, name each scout and the provider that gets its prompt, Anthropic; that notice authorizes the send.
- In `ce-code-review`, `ce-doc-review`, and `ce-simplify-code`, ask each reviewer and peer for the skill's findings schema only, and re-read only what you need to confirm a finding.

# Subagents

- When your own subagent tool can run Claude models, such as Claude Code's `Agent`, launch every Claude subagent through that tool, including the scouts, verifiers, reviewers, and workers a skill dispatches, and pass the role's alias as `model`, even when `orchestrator_capabilities` shows nonempty `inheritedOptions` and even where T3 Code's own guidance prefers `delegate_task` for same-provider work.
- Launch through `delegate_task` only what that tool cannot run, such as the Codex review peer, and the Claude subagents of a session whose own subagent tool cannot run Claude models, such as a Codex, OpenCode, or Pi session inside T3 Code, targeting the `claudeAgent` instance with the model ID, a `ce-work` worker's effort as `effort` in `target.options` when its category names one, and no other `target.options`.
- When the session lists no T3 Code delegation tools, use your harness's own subagent tool.
- When no skill or rule assigns a model to a subagent, launch it as `sonnet`, `claude-sonnet-5-5` on the `claudeAgent` instance, and use `opus` and `fable` only where a skill or these rules name them.
- A fresh subagent sees none of your conversation, so put everything it needs in its prompt, including that it stays read-only when it only searches, reads, or reviews.
- When your own subagent tool offers a `fork` mode, launch each reviewer and verifier fresh, never as a `fork`, so it does not start from your own conclusion.
- Never put a file under `secrets/` or a value you know to be decrypted or a credential into a subagent's prompt.
- Launch a unit that needs its own checkout with `isolation: "worktree"` on your own subagent tool when it offers that argument, such as Claude Code's `Agent`, and otherwise, when the session lists T3 Code's delegation tools, with `isolation: "worktree"` on `delegate_task`, name the parent commit in its prompt for it to verify before it edits, and tell it to commit its edits and report its branch.
- Before you edit a file that a still-running subagent also edits, in this checkout or in a worktree whose branch you will fast-forward into this checkout, wait until that subagent has finished and its edits are merged.
- Never run two subagents that edit the same files in one checkout at the same time: from a clean checkout, launch each in its own worktree as the worktree rule above says, tell each to commit its edits and report its branch, and bring those branches back one at a time by rebasing each onto the tip the previous fast-forward left and merging it with `git merge --ff-only`; otherwise launch them one after another.
- Launch a subagent in its own worktree, as the worktree rule above says, when it runs a command that rewrites files beyond the subagent's own, such as a formatter, a code generator, a dependency update, a branch switch, or a mutation test run inside the checkout, tell it to commit its edits and report its branch, and bring that branch back once it has finished by rebasing it onto your current branch and merging it with `git merge --ff-only`.
- Launch a subagent in its own worktree, as the worktree rule above says, when it needs the committed state while your checkout holds uncommitted edits it must not see, such as a baseline build or a test run against the last commit.
- Tell subagents that share one checkout not to commit, stage, or format files beyond their own, and, while another subagent edits there, to leave formatting, builds, and checks to you; commit their results yourself.
- After you bring that branch back with `git merge --ff-only` without a conflict, remove its worktree with `git worktree remove` and its branch with `git branch -d`, never with `--force` or `-D`; when the rebase conflicts, run `git rebase --abort` and keep the worktree and the branch.
- Run that rebase with `git rebase` inside the branch's worktree, because a branch checked out in a worktree can be rebased only there, and only once the subagent has finished and its worktree has no uncommitted changes. When `git merge --ff-only` is refused because your tip moved, rebase once more and retry; when it is refused for any other reason, such as uncommitted edits in your checkout, keep the worktree and the branch and report them, and never use `git stash`, `--autostash`, or `git reset` to get past it.
- Use `git cherry-pick` only when you take some but not all of a task branch's commits, and then keep that branch and report it as holding unmerged commits.
- When a run used worktrees, end your report by naming each worktree or branch it leaves with uncommitted changes or unmerged commits, and for a kept native worktree name its branch as what survives, because removing the parent T3 Code worktree deletes the kept checkout and leaves the branch, and say that its commits stay on that branch and can be checked out again with `git worktree add <path> <branch>`.
- Tell each subagent to run the commands its report depends on in the foreground, raising a slow command's timeout as far as its harness allows and waiting inside the turn for one that outlasts it, and not to end its turn until it has written its final report, because the last message of that turn is its result; ending the turn while such a command still runs in the background and an ending that promises a later report, such as "I'll report when it finishes", are forbidden to it, while a server it keeps running for its own work, such as a dev server for a browser check, may stay in the background.
- Tell each subagent to give a command that can take more than a minute, such as a test suite, a build, or a flake check, a timeout that covers its expected run before it starts the command, where its shell tool takes one and up to the tool's limit, such as 600000 ms for Claude Code's Bash tool, whose 120000 ms default moves a longer command to the background.
- Tell it that when its harness moves a command it waits on to the background, it waits for that command inside the turn with blocking foreground calls, each within the tool's limit, until the harness's record of that one command shows it finished, and then reads the command's output and exit status from that record, such as with a Claude Code Bash loop like `until grep -q '^\[exited with code' <output-file>; do sleep 5; done` on the output file the Bash tool named, under a `timeout` below 600000 ms and repeated as needed, never with a match on a process name, which can catch another task's run, because Claude Code's `Monitor` and background notifications do not hold the turn, whatever its tool descriptions advise.
- Tell it never to stop and rerun a background command it waits on, whether its harness moved the command there or it started the command there on purpose, because a missing or short timeout does not make the command wrong, and to stop one only when it was the wrong command, when it is still running at twice the time it expected, or when a time limit its task prompt sets arrives, and then to report what it has; a command its harness killed at the timeout it reruns once with a timeout that covers the run, with none where its tool allows that, or, when no timeout can cover it, in the background on purpose as below.
- Tell it to start a command it expects to outlast its shell tool's foreground limit in the background on purpose instead of in the foreground, through the tool's own background option, such as `run_in_background` on Claude Code's Bash tool, rather than a shell `&` that the harness can kill when the call returns, and to wait for it the same way, never ending its turn while the command runs; a server it keeps running for its own work is not such a command.

# T3 Code

- When the session lists T3 Code's pull request tools, such as `watch_pull_request`, use them to watch a request you babysit instead of your own background wait.
- When the session lists T3 Code's preview tools, such as `preview_open`, show any local HTML file you open for the user in a T3 Code browser tab instead of running `xdg-open` or `open`: run `serve-html <file>`, open a new tab with `preview_open` and `reuseExistingTab` set to false so the user's own tab stays as it is, then call `preview_navigate` with that tab's `tabId` and the line `serve-html` printed as `target`. Pass `--root <dir>` to `serve-html` when the page loads sibling files from a report directory. When the user asks to see a page again, or says its tab no longer loads, run `serve-html` again instead of reusing an earlier target, and navigate the same tab to the new target. A run with no person present, such as a headless or pipeline run, still opens nothing. When the session lists no T3 Code preview tools, or `serve-html`, `preview_open`, or `preview_navigate` fails, keep the skill's own way of opening the file and print the file's absolute path.

## Delegated tasks

- The rules under this heading apply only to tasks you launch with `delegate_task`, and the rules in the Subagents section cover every subagent.
- A delegated task has none of the tool limits its native agent type had, so state the limits it needs in its task prompt.
- Tell each delegated task to launch no subagents of its own, so delegation stays one level deep.
- Launch every delegated task, cross-model peers and implementation units included, with `runtimeMode: "full-access"`, even though `delegate_task`'s own description says to leave it unset, so the task never pauses on an approval nobody sees.
- When `delegate_task` rejects that launch with an error about the requested runtime mode, such as one saying the target provider does not offer it or that it ranks above your thread's mode, relaunch the task once without `runtimeMode`, so it inherits your thread's mode, under a new `clientRequestId`; that rejection itself is not a failed or unavailable launch under any rule in these instructions, including the launch-failure retry below and a failed cross-model peer, but a failure of the relaunch is one.
- When a `delegate_task` launch fails, relaunch it once with the same `clientRequestId`; when that fails too, do the work yourself, unless these instructions forbid it, as they do for a cross-model peer; a worktree launch of a task other than a cross-model peer that still fails after that relaunch is skipped and reported with the reason when the task needed the committed state or must rewrite files beyond its own, and runs in your checkout once every other task there has finished only when it got its worktree for parallel edits of one file.
- Launch every delegated task that does not need another task's result in the same response, each with `delegate_task` in its default `async` mode, instead of launching one, waiting for it, and then launching the next.
- Never use `mode: "wait"`, even for a single task, because it holds your turn until the task finishes.
- Launch every delegated task other than a cross-model peer in the checkout you work in, with no `isolation` argument, and give a task its own worktree only in the cases the worktree rules of the Subagents section name; a cross-model peer is the one task that always gets a worktree, as the peer rule above says.
- Give each delegated task its own `clientRequestId`, and reuse it when you retry that launch, except for the relaunch without `runtimeMode` after a rejected runtime mode, which takes a new one.
- Launch a task that needs an earlier task's result only after you have read that result.
- In a run with a person present, after launching, do the work that does not need the delegated results, and when only those results remain, end your turn so the user can talk to you while the tasks run.
- When you end your turn while tasks still run, name them, and when one of them runs without `runtimeMode: "full-access"`, also say that such a task can pause on an approval, which shows "Subagent needs approval" in its thread and waits for the user, because T3 Code sends no notification for it.
- Each notification T3 Code delivers, whether it starts your turn or arrives with a user message, names the tasks that reached a terminal state or have a question; read each finished task's result with one `task_status` call.
- While another task the waiting step needs is still running, end your turn again without cancelling it, and continue that step once every result it needs has arrived through a notification or from the task's session.
- When a notification says a delegated task has a question, read it with `t3_pending_request_read`, then answer it with `t3_pending_request_respond` or ask the user, and end your turn again.
- When the user writes while delegated tasks are running, send any answer they give to a pending question to its task with `t3_pending_request_respond`, answer the rest, keep the tasks and the waiting step, and end your turn again unless they redirect the work.
- On every turn you get while a task the waiting step needs has not reached a terminal state and you have not taken its result, whether a notification, a user message, a background wait, or a `t3_thread_wait` timeout started it, read its `task_status` once, then read its session with `t3_thread_read` on its `childThreadId` in the `activity` view, paging with `afterPosition` until no `nextPosition` remains, and read its latest assistant message by `itemId`, repeating with `textOffset` set to `nextTextOffset` until `nextTextOffset` is null, because a task can write its result and never finish.
- When that `task_status` is terminal and shows `hasPendingChildRuns` false, use its result as usual, reading `latestTerminalSummary` in place of `summary` when it is not null, because it holds a later turn's result; when that result only promises a later report, read its session as above and use a finished message there that holds the whole result, and otherwise count the task failed and say that it ended without its report; when it shows a pending question or `hasPendingChildRuns` true, keep waiting.
- T3 Code sends no notification when a later turn of a terminal task ends, so in a run with a person present, when you end your turn while a task the waiting step needs is terminal with `hasPendingChildRuns` true, name it and tell the user that no notification will come for its later turn, so they write to you once that turn has finished, or stop the task when its thread shows no progress.
- Otherwise, when the latest assistant message is finished, holds the whole result the task prompt asked for, and nothing in the session has changed since your previous check of it, use the message as the task's result, stop the task with `task_cancel`, and say that you took its result from the session.
- When `task_cancel` leaves that task running, keep the result, tell the user once that it still runs so they can stop it, and count its result as taken.
- In a run with a person present, never wait for a delegated task with `t3_thread_wait`; in any run, never call `task_status` in a loop, and never start a sleep, timer, or background command whose only purpose is to wake yourself later. A delay a test must reproduce, a retry interval, a wait the user asked for, a wait that blocks on the task's own run, and a foreground wait inside the turn on a command you run stay allowed.
- Ending your turn to wait for a delegated task is allowed even where a skill says not to end the turn or to fail when no blocking wait exists, such as `lfg`'s rule not to end the turn before `<promise>DONE</promise>` or `ce-code-review`'s reviewer collection; when the results arrive, continue the same run from the step that waited.
- For such a skill, a completion notification and one `task_status` read that shows `hasPendingChildRuns` false, or a result taken from the task's session, are the task's terminal outcome, and a task the skill bounds by time counts as failed when you resume after that bound has passed and its session holds no whole result.
- In a run with no person present, such as a headless, scheduled, or pipeline run, wait inside the turn instead: once every task is launched, call `t3_thread_wait` on each task's `childThreadId` with its `childRunId` as `runId` and, as `timeoutMs`, the smaller of 600000 and the time left before any bound on that task, then read each result with one `task_status` call; when no time is left before that bound, skip the wait and apply the bound rule below.
- When that wait returns `timedOut: true`, answer a pending question with `t3_pending_request_respond` from the task and your instructions, check the task as above, and wait again with the same `runId` unless you took its result or its bound has passed.
- In a run with no person present, when a task's `task_status` shows `pendingApproval`, nobody can approve it, so cancel it with `task_cancel` and count it as failed.
- When a skill bounds a delegated task by time and the bound passes while it still runs, check it as above, and when its latest finished assistant message holds the whole result, take it as above even if the session changed since your previous check; otherwise cancel it with `task_cancel` and count it as failed.
- When `task_status` shows `workState` `working` or `hasPendingChildRuns` true, call `t3_thread_wait` on that thread again without `runId` and with `timeoutMs` as above; when `childRunId` is null, the task shows `waiting_for_children` with `hasPendingChildRuns` false, or a wait without `runId` returns at once without `timedOut` while the task is still terminal with `hasPendingChildRuns` true, there is no run to block on, so check it as above, take a finished message that holds the whole result, count the task failed only when the bound has already passed at that check and the session holds no whole result, and otherwise end your turn with no timer and no further check, and name the tasks when a person is present.
- Once you have read a delegated task's result or counted the task failed, cancelled ones included, settle its thread with `t3_thread_organize` and action `settle`, and never settle your own thread.
